  "answer": "Myopia, also known as nearsightedness, is a condition where a person can see nearby objects clearly but has difficulty seeing distant objects. This occurs because the eye's lens focuses light from distant objects in front of the retina instead of directly on it. To correct myopia, a concave lens (also called a diverging lens) is used. This lens spreads out the incoming light rays before they enter the eye, allowing the eye's lens to focus the image correctly on the retina. A ray diagram for myopia correction shows that the concave lens takes the parallel rays from a distant object and bends them so that they appear to come from a point within the person's far point, making the image clear on the retina.",
